        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best balance board?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on what you want it for. For a standing desk, a low, gentle board such as the FluidStance Plane, or a cheap rocker like the StrongTek wooden board. For a first board at home, a rocker and then a wobble board such as the Yes4All. For surf or snowboard practice, a roller board such as the Indo Board Original or the Revbalance 101 V2.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between a wobble board and a rocker board?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A rocker board tips along one axis, front to back or side to side, so it is easier to control and a good first board. A wobble board sits on a dome and tilts in every direction, which is harder and asks more of the ankles and hips. Roller boards, which travel sideways on a cylinder, are harder still.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are balance boards good for standing desks?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A low, gentle board can make standing at a desk more comfortable by letting you shift your weight, and many people like that. Choose a desk board or rocker, not a roller board, check that your desk goes high enough to make up for the board's height, and alternate it with sitting and standing on the floor. Seller claims about calorie burn are the seller's own.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are balance boards safe?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They are designed to be unstable, so falling is the main risk. Clear space around the board, start next to a wall or sturdy support, begin with an easy design, and use roller boards on carpet with the stops close together. Talk to a doctor or physical therapist first if you have a balance problem, dizziness, a history of falls, osteoporosis, a recent injury or are pregnant.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a balance board help with balance problems?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              We cannot tell you that, and you should not use a board to treat unsteadiness yourself. Balance problems have causes that need proper assessment, so see a doctor or physical therapist first. If they recommend balance work, they can tell you whether a board is appropriate and how to use it safely. This page is not medical advice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much weight can a balance board hold?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It varies by board and maker. Our picks are rated from 300 lb for the Yes4All wobble board to 400 lb for the Revbalance 101 V2, with the StrongTek and Indo boards at 350 lb. Listings for the same board sometimes disagree, so check the maker's current page and stay below the limit, especially if you hold weights.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

